# --- Morgan Lloyd CRM (Dynamics 365) -----------------------------------------
# Credentials are issued by Morgan Lloyd (crmsupport@clifton-asset.co.uk):
# an Azure directory (tenant), a registered app (client id) and its secret.
MORGAN_LLOYD_RESOURCE_URL=https://cliftonassetdev.crm11.dynamics.com
MORGAN_LLOYD_TENANT_ID=your-azure-tenant-guid
MORGAN_LLOYD_CLIENT_ID=your-app-client-id
MORGAN_LLOYD_CLIENT_SECRET=your-app-client-secret

# Fixed reference values the vendor issues per integrating firm, in "Morgan Lloyd API Details-
# Graphene.docx" — none is discoverable from the API. The Create Scheme pairing is validated as a
# PAIR ("Invalid Scheme and Sipp Type combination"), and both "Firm ID we provided you" values are
# Dynamics GUIDs. Blank means an operation that needs one refuses naming it rather than sending a
# plausible wrong value. Clifton dev org: 16 / 809130016 / 6ac39921-d8b7-f011-bbd3-7ced8d6b85ff.
# ADVISER_FIRM_ID and INVESTMENT_PROVIDER_ID are the SAME GUID (the vendor asks for it under
# three field names); setting the first is enough. Neither is MORGAN_LLOYD_TENANT_ID, which is
# the Azure directory rather than a Dynamics `accounts` row.
#
# ADVISER_CONTACT_ID is a FOURTH identifier and required TOGETHER with the firm: a
# contact attached to a firm with no adviser contact is refused outright ('Financial
# Adviser contact must be populated for the adviser firm'). It is a person, not an
# account, and both are sent as entity references (`/contacts(...)`, `/accounts(...)`) —
# a bare GUID is 'Invalid Format'. Discoverable from the firm via
# cam_getadvisercontacts, so blank is fine when the firm has exactly one adviser.
MORGAN_LLOYD_ADVISER_FIRM_ID=
MORGAN_LLOYD_ADVISER_CONTACT_ID=
MORGAN_LLOYD_SCHEME_TYPE=
MORGAN_LLOYD_SIPP_TYPE=
MORGAN_LLOYD_INVESTMENT_PROVIDER_ID=

# --- Sync ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Adviser email addresses whose books are pulled via cam_getadvisercontacts
# (comma-separated). Blank skips the adviser-book models.
ML_SYNC_ADVISER_EMAILS=
# --- Warehouse (DuckLake: catalogue in the datalakes Postgres DB, parquet in the
# core bucket's datalake zone — docs/datalake.md). Local stack values shown.

# ── Shared object store + warehouse: set these ONCE in the ROOT `.env`, not here ───────────────
# Every provider service writes parquet into the same core bucket and catalogues it in the same
# `datalakes` Postgres, so these are canonical names the API and the compose stack already use:
#
#   DATALAKE_WRITER_DSN   libpq DSN for lake_writer, e.g.
#                         "dbname=datalakes host=localhost port=5432 user=lake_writer password=lake_writer"
#   S3_CORE_BUCKET        platforms
#   S3_ENDPOINT_HOST      localhost:9000   (host[:port], no scheme — the DuckDB secret's form)
#   S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID      rustfsadmin
#   S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY  rustfsadmin
#
# The moon tasks read `/.env` then this file, so a value here still overrides the shared one when a
# provider genuinely needs its own store. It previously had to: each service asked for its own
# `ML_WAREHOUSE_DSN` / `ML_S3_*`, so one value lived in three files and a port change meant
# three edits. Worth removing because of how the mistake fails, not because it is untidy: a wrong
# endpoint does not raise — the parquet lands where the reader never looks, and it presents as
# "the catalogue is empty".
